{"kind":"propose","text":"Authorize the following bounded proposed-profile P1d investigation using the freshly audited immutable P1c input described in the direct audit above and the live governing Plan001. This explicitly replaces my680 direction on inherited stale artifact lineage; the failed refresh and historical lineage remain visible. No implementation requirement or confidentiality boundary changes. The actual P1c landing prerequisite is satisfied at1d4d775b342a34a32ca1690c461071b7010a9282; all references below to waiting for that landing are fulfilled. This is ordinary proposal-and-ratification authority to conduct development feasibility, not adoption through a stale authority-bearing artifact chain and not production crypto adoption.\n\nBounded next-feasibility direction for G2-LONG-HISTORY: preserve full newcomer application history for the supported admitted history and Plan001's longer-history/10,000-small-event target. Select independently keyed sealed plaintext intervals with linked owner-signed manifests, a named full-prefix checkpoint, a snapshot-specific bounded key-grant bundle and a compact separately encrypted owner vault as the next PROPOSED recovery profile. Assessment441/a3fde564edaab9777ac081dc4adee5dd74a39030 is requester-ratified as advice; its G2-S01–S06 supplies the detailed implementation/test contract. This direction does not adopt production crypto or mark any gate passed.\n\nProceed only AFTER the current P1c correction has completed fresh independent exact-head review and guarded landing. Root will issue a separate bounded continuation request from its actual live landed artifacts; do not fold this into the currently frozen correction, overlap promises, or treat unreviewed c3ff4c921d5e7609007b8d793a5f4d5662b3d99a as an approved base. Current P1C-04/05 ordinary failure findings must be resolved, including exact-identity reservations before fallible writes and learned-control denial before retention. The original G1-G5 STOP, evidence, review/adoption and P2-P9 boundaries remain.\n\nProposed representation: introduce a new explicit recovery-profile version, retaining exact MLS/provider/Nostr pins unless separately justified and reviewed. Seal nonempty plaintext intervals at128 ordered entries or16MiB canonical segment plaintext, whichever first; include every ordered control/stale/refusal entry, exact original signed ciphertext/action and message-specific opening closure, with explicit entry/application/byte counts. The129th entry starts another interval. Single-entry plus mandatory proof/context overflow refuses, never skips. Keep one immutable separately encrypted64-file/512KiB definition closure. Do not repeat the entire prior history/definition in every segment or export the live MLS/owner vault to history readers.\n\nFreeze an acyclic canonical byte graph before testing: interval BodyCore and public boundary state hashes -\u003e context/range/predecessor/previous-manifest/body/length/key/nonce SegmentMeta -\u003e independently encrypted ciphertext -\u003e exact16KiB transport chunk events bound to metaId/index/count -\u003e owner-signed manifest of metadata/chunk IDs/ciphertext hash -\u003e named full-prefix checkpoint committing last manifest, exact coverage/tip/counts/definition descriptor and compact private-vault core hash. No chunk/AAD input can name a later/self-referential manifest/checkpoint. Define actual new kinds/tags/domains after checking the pinned primary NIP registry; do not silently reinterpret existing v1 encrypted bytes.\n\nEach segment uses a fresh independent random key/nonce; once sealed or granted, never extend it with future plaintext. New full-grant bundles use new independent grant keys when they add segment keys. The small signed Nostr/NIP44 delivery binds authorized sender/recipient/context/checkpoint/grant locator; verify the signed outer event before decryption. Shared grants contain exactly the selected checkpoint's segment keys and definition key, never owner-vault/private MLS continuation/future keys. Compact owner recovery contains accepted MLS/device/account/leaf state, final public state, archive/grant binding and only non-derivable continuation material; reconstruct historical indexes/outcomes from fully verified history rather than embedding a giant prior Snapshot. Support settled checkpoints (pending=null) explicitly; exporting unresolved pending operations is not inferred. The declared all-device-loss package must suffice without lost live objects.\n\nPreserve trust and privacy: the owner attests historical encrypted control-transition/public-context validity over the entire named interval; clients still independently reopen every actual application ciphertext and verify its MLS/account/action correspondence. This is not independent historical MLS Commit replay. Old copied openings remain usable; rewrapping is not revocation; old owner-vault and immutable owner authority compromise remain explicit. Keep the chosen highest-verified-retained-prefix gateway policy, including previously accepted unseen-removal old-history exposure, known-control/send/cursor restrictions and restore fences. Final adoption must affirm these trust/exposure choices against real evidence again.\n\nFull recovery exists only at SEALED checkpoint boundaries. Separate gateway authorization frontierF, requested recovery tipT and complete retained closure; require T\u003c=F, and no segment/range/chunk extending beyondT orF is released merely because its locator is accessible. A mirror ending inside a later segment may offer the prior complete sealed checkpoint, accurately labelling the remaining suffix unavailable. A valid old checkpoint is rejected when the caller requires a newer one. No EOSE, owner-signed false manifest or public retention proof substitutes for client plaintext verification. Preserve128-event/1MiB pages and131200-byte full-message limits; use bounded exact-ID descriptor/chunk pages and a small actual multi-page gateway/strfry/newcomer experiment, not raw backend access.\n\nChoose conservative count dimensions for this next feasibility candidate:65,536 total ordered entries and65,536 nonempty segments, alongside the existing256MiB aggregate required retained signed-byte quota and64MiB outbox. These are checked safety ceilings for the proposal, not guarantees that maximal32KiB actions fit those bytes or silently adopted production service limits. Keep16MiB canonical full-grant bound and explicitly bound compact descriptor-index allocation and global logical map by measured encoding/count limits; never hold every full manifest/segment in memory. Measure exact max/+1 key-map/manifest/chunk/frame totals before calling them feasible. Proposed count/byte adjustments must be justified and freshly reviewed, preserving10,000-small-event capability. Physical disk/process persistence/performance remain their later owners.\n\nRecoverability admission must be explicit: reserve actual prepared closure costs or a proven conservative public-input bound, including all required retained orders/segments/manifests/definition/grant/checkpoint objects and uncertain writes, BEFORE making the stronger recoverable-prefix promise. Do not wait until journal storage is exhausted and then discover it cannot hold the promised archive. Distinguish an ordinary opaque signed ordering receipt from an authenticated, exported and retained full-history checkpoint. If valid required closure cannot fit, refuse the corresponding stronger advancement and stop accepting work under that guarantee; preserve the last completeT. Invalid opaque input may still stall at the existing last-good frontier, not be privately skipped. Freeze and exercise this eligibility/refusal state machine and identify any remaining production transaction coupling honestly; no purported full G2 adoption if the admitted-history promise is still ambiguous.\n\nWork must be approximately linear per complete export/replay, with a single decoded segment plus bounded closure/public state, indexed opening lookup and a compact bounded descriptor/logical index. Do not clone or re-encrypt accumulated history on ordinary append or every receipt. Sealing adds only a new interval and descriptor. Generating a requested full snapshot grant/export may legitimately visit all selected keys once; it must not become mandatory O(N) reissuance on every ordinary entry/segment append. Make the distinction between ordinary append, publishing an explicit full recovery checkpoint, and recipient-specific grant generation concrete; measure their separate work. No claim of streaming AEAD while a one-shot WebCrypto call holds the full segment. No SQL or production fold/UI requirement introduced.\n\nMinimum evidence: real Node and Chromium, more than128 total entries across at least3 nonempty intervals, at least2 epoch changes with controls inside/at boundaries, refusal and cross-segment exact re-encryption duplicate/conflict, authentic original-event/opening verification, fresh Welcome/newcomer full recovery and independent declared all-device-loss restore with real future send. Cover all G2-S06 re-signed coverage/root/count/key/old-checkpoint/interval/manifest/extra-future-key negatives, actual segment/grant/frame/count/capacity boundaries, immutable prior bytes on append and no future access from an old grant within the same epoch, actual removed-holder negative plus retained-member positive. A small real gateway/native test must transfer multiple intervals/pages, verify the newcomer grant/archive, reject wrong/missing/beyondF objects and removal between pages. Interrupted sealing/transfer cannot report complete. Add evidence rejection for every new mandatory case, source/profile/lock/library/native/fixture/frontier identity and retained failed attempts.\n\nDo not demand10,000-event benchmarking, resumable production downloads, fsync/IndexedDB, actual DO/mirror deployment or generic app UI in this continuation; those stay in P4-P9. Do require the exact composition/custody/bounds/eligibility contract and its discriminating feasibility evidence before considering G2 and protocol adoption. Actual later independent review and explicit root adoption plus a separately reviewed detached-decision validator are still required to activate full gate commands. Main's new live artifacts will anchor that work after P1c lands; this is a reviewable next-step direction only.","body":{"input_snapshot":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:6443fb0213ed72d0443526bcc909ed841b29b110","scope":"P1d development feasibility only; no production adoption"}}